A||||8||Shawn Alexander||Wheels and tires
Z||000000||Shawn Alexander||03-01-99||09:24 AM||||I am about to buy new 15" wheels<br>and tires for my stock bodied<br>fiero that is lowered about 1".<br>My question is what would be the <br>widest wheel and tire i can go<br>without scrubbing the wheel well<br>and what would be the correct <br>back spacing.
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||03-01-99||10:11 AM||||I had my Fiero lowered with about 2-3" (which btw looks absolutely fantastic!) and I have 205/60/15 all around. I could probably go to 215 but that would be it!
Z||000002||raceknb||03-01-99||08:58 PM||||If you check the fitment guide at your local tire store, you will see the the GT's came with 15" wheels standard and came with a staggar in the tire sizes.  205/60r15 ft and 215/60r15 rr.  The optimum handeling package is 225/50r15 all the way around.
Z||000003||Shawn Alexander||03-03-99||04:03 PM||||raceknb,<br>  My main concern is tire rube (being<br>that the car is an inch or so lower<br>than stock). Do you really think i<br>would have the clearance needed for<br>225/50s?
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||03-03-99||04:38 PM||||Shawn, I checked my car today to see if  215/60 (instead of the 205/60 I have now) would fit and to be honest, I don't think it will. There isn't much space left between the tire and the body above it... I'll see if I can take a picture of it and show you what I mean.<p>On the other hand, 215/50 or 225/50 *would* probably fit because those tires are much lower.
Z||000005||Shawn Alexander||03-04-99||08:48 AM||||Cliff,<br>   A close up pic would be great.<br>This would give me a better idea<br>of what i'm dealing with.<br>Thanks,<br>Shawn
Z||000006||raceknb||03-06-99||04:20 PM||||I currently am an SCCA autocrosser and I compete agianst several othe Fiero owners. One gentleman I race against has done the 308 body conversion and riding 1 1/2 inches lower has managed to squeeze 296/50r 15 under the rear, and 255/50r 15 in the front.<br>Suspension travel on these cars is minimal and since you have lowered your car 1"  you have cut down on the distance the car can travel.  225/50r15 will clear with no trouble.  I currently am testing a set of 245/45zr16 under the rear and 225/50zr16 under the front.  I am not lowered at all, but I do have large tires for the back end.  So far...Not a single rub and I sstill have almost 1/2 inch off the rear strut housing.  A rear coil over conversion will allow unlimited tire sizing. 1 of the cars that shows up from time to time is set on 275/40zr17 in ther rear (yes  a corvette tire under a fiero)  any other questions  e-mail at raceknb@sprintmail.com.  I love to hear from other fiero owners.
Z||000007||Tyler||03-11-99||09:52 AM||||I was at the Toronto Fiero Club's show this summer and there was a gentleman there who had a pair of 235/50/15s on the rear of a stock fiero.  Real close fit, but he claims that there was no rubbing.  When I looked, I didn't see any evidence on the back of the tires either.
Z||000008||cmore||03-11-99||07:34 PM||||I'm currently running 245/45/16 on the rear and 225/50/16 on the front. I have lowered it 2 1/2" so the front tires do rub on the fenders. It has cut through some of the inner plastic fender well but this reason isn't a major concern. Wide tires with a good 4 wheel alignment make a really big difference in the handling of a Fiero. I can our corner almost any car on the road but the ride is very rough. I'm using 450lbs springs with koni shocks and stuts at max pressure. Also I have a 1 1/4" stainless steel rear sway bar in the rear. All of this helps me win most of my SCCA Solo 2 races which is always fun to beat the pants off a v8. :)
